  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Consider the vector field $F=(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi y)\\,i+(ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi y)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi y,\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$",
